@page "/help"

<PageTitle>Help</PageTitle>

<h1>Help</h1>

<p class="lead">
    Store once. Seed everywhere. Waste nothing.
</p>

<p>
    Simplifiarr finds duplicate copies of your media between your download
    folders and your libraries, and lets you replace the wasted copy with an
    NTFS hardlink instead - so the file exists once on disk but shows up in
    both places. It also finds completed downloads that haven't been added to
    a library yet ("promotion opportunities") and can hardlink them in.
    Nothing is ever copied between drives, and nothing is deleted without
    going through Quarantine first.
</p>

<div class="card mb-3">
    <div class="card-body">
        <h3>Home</h3>

        <p>
            The dashboard is read-only - it doesn't scan anything itself. Every
            number on it reflects whatever the Hardlinks and Potential Waste
            pages last found. Click a card to jump to the page that
            produced that number and run a fresh scan there.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-3">
    <div class="card-body">
        <h3>Drive Profiles (Settings)</h3>

        <p>
            Before scanning anything, set up a Drive Profile for each physical
            drive you use. A profile groups together the paths that live on
            one drive: its download folder, and its Movie/TV/Book/Audiobook/Music/Comic
            library folders. This matters because hardlinks only work between two
            paths on the <strong>same</strong> drive - Simplifiarr will never
            copy a file across drives to "fix" this.
        </p>

        <p>
            Go to <strong>Settings</strong>, click <strong>Add Drive Profile</strong>,
            and fill in:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>Drive Root</strong> - e.g. <code>C:\</code> or <code>E:\</code></li>
            <li><strong>Download Path</strong> - where completed torrents land</li>
            <li><strong>Movie Library / TV Library / Book Library / Audiobook Library / Music Library / Comic Library</strong> - your organized library folders (leave any blank if you don't use that library on this drive)</li>
            <li><strong>Audiobook Download Path / Music Download Path / Comic Download Path</strong> - dedicated download-side folders for audiobooks, music, and comics. Audiobook and music files share the same formats (MP3/M4A/M4B/FLAC) and can't be told apart by content alone, so each needs its own download subfolder to scan; comics get a dedicated folder too so classification never depends on guesswork - leave blank to skip scanning for that type</li>
            <li><strong>Seed Only Path</strong> - optional, for torrents you only keep for seeding</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            Each path shows <span style="color:green">Exists</span> or
            <span style="color:red">Missing</span> as you type, so you can
            catch typos before saving. Click <strong>Save Settings</strong> when done -
            if the save fails (usually a file-permission issue on
            <code>C:\ProgramData\Simplifiarr\settings.json</code>), you'll see a red
            banner explaining why.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-3">
    <div class="card-body">
        <h3>Hardlinks (Promotion Opportunities)</h3>

        <p>
            This page finds completed downloads that aren't in a library yet
            and lets you hardlink them in. Each drive gets its own card:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>Scan Drive</strong> - scans Movies, then TV, then Books, then Audiobooks, then Music, then Comics for that drive, one after another</li>
            <li><strong>Scan Movies / Scan TV / Scan Books / Scan Audiobooks / Scan Music / Scan Comics</strong> - scan just that one library. Scanning one never touches the cached results of the others, so you can refresh just what changed.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>
            <strong>Movies</strong>, <strong>Books</strong>,
            <strong>Audiobooks</strong>, <strong>Music</strong>, and <strong>Comics</strong> show one row per
            item. <strong>TV</strong> groups episodes by show and season -
            if you have a season pack sitting in your downloads and only some
            episodes are missing from your library, you'll see one card per
            show/season with a "Promote All N Episode(s)" button, plus an
            expandable list to promote individual episodes instead.
        </p>

        <p>
            Clicking <strong>Promote</strong> shows a preview of exactly what
            will happen (source file, target path, planned actions) before
            anything is touched - nothing happens until you click
            <strong>Confirm Promotion</strong>.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-3">
    <div class="card-body">
        <h3>Potential Waste</h3>

        <p>
            This page finds files that already exist in <em>both</em> your
            download folder and your library as separate copies - storage
            being wasted on a duplicate. It uses the same per-drive,
            per-library (Movies/TV) scan buttons as Hardlinks.
        </p>

        <p>
            Each result can be converted individually, or you can check
            several and click <strong>Convert Selected</strong> to batch-process
            them one at a time. Converting a file:
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>Moves the existing library copy to Quarantine (nothing is deleted yet)</li>
            <li>Creates an NTFS hardlink in its place pointing at the download copy</li>
            <li>Verifies the hardlink actually took effect before calling it a success</li>
        </ol>

        <p>
            Files already hardlinked (no waste) don't show up in this list at
            all - that count feeds the "Healthy Hardlinks" number on Home
            instead.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-3">
    <div class="card-body">
        <h3>Quarantine</h3>

        <p>
            Every file a conversion displaces lands here instead of being
            deleted immediately. Review what's in Quarantine and delete it
            permanently once you're confident the hardlink is working
            correctly - that's the point where the space is actually
            reclaimed (and shows up as "Storage Saved" on Home).
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-3">
    <div class="card-body">
        <h3>History</h3>

        <p>
            An audit log of every conversion, promotion, and permanent
            deletion Simplifiarr has performed, with totals for conversions,
            deletes, and reclaimed space. <strong>Clear History</strong> permanently
            erases this log - it asks for confirmation first, and it does not
            undo any conversions or deletions themselves, only the record of
            them.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-3">
    <div class="card-body">
        <h3>Tips</h3>

        <ul>
            <li>Scans read every file in the folders you configure, so the first scan of a large library can take a while - later scans of the same folder within a page reuse what was already found where possible.</li>
            <li>If a scan button seems stuck, give it a bit longer before assuming something's wrong - real disk scanning takes real time.</li>
            <li>Hardlinks require the source and target to be on the same NTFS volume. If a path is on the wrong drive for its profile, promotion/conversion will be blocked rather than silently copying the file.</li>
            <li>Quarantine is your safety net - nothing is permanently deleted until you delete it from Quarantine yourself.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>
